SM(470,{"n":"Acts 16","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 16.mp3","d":3274.4,"s":[[0.4,"Come and study Holy Scripture with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.4,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed tradition, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.16,"So uh when we get started here, in just a moment, we're gonna be starting in Acts the Apostles, chapter 16."],[29.92,"Um as I usually say at uh the beginning of these, by the time people are hearing this on the internet, the recording, uh the recording of all the other ones, including the first one, uh, will already be up there, and you'll be able to go back and listen to uh my introduction to the book of Acts."],[48.0,"I'll also apologize to all of you and to the poor fella at Ancient Faith Radio who's gonna be editing this later for all the coughing I'm gonna do over the course of the evening."],[57.44,"Uh but can't help it."],[59.92,"Trust me, if I could stop, I would"],[63.72,"But so to get us caught up to where we are right now, where we left off."],[68.84,"We've been reading about the beginnings of first St."],[73.88,"Paul and Barnabas together."],[77.64,"Their first missionary journey together, where they went to several cities."],[83.88,"They went first to Cyprus and then to several cities in Asia Minor, what's now Turkey, and preached the gospel there and started churches in those cities."],[94.52,"And then we read that they were preparing to go on another journey, and there was a little bit of a falling out, and so the two separated and, and uh Saint Paul took uh Silas with him, uh Saint Barnabas took uh Saint John Mark, who, as we mentioned before, is the uh Saint Mark who wrote the gospel, according to Saint Mark, uh took him, and they sort of went on two separate two separate journeys."],[124.4,"Um Saint Barnabas and uh Saint Mark went back to Cyprus as we saw earlier in the book of Acts."],[132.08,"Uh Saint Barnabas was from Cyprus and had been part of the Jewish community there before he had heard the gospel, and so he's now going back to bring the gospel to uh his Jewish community there."],[145.52,"And uh Saints Paul and Silas are getting ready to go this S toilas the."],[150.12,"Where we heard about Silas, I think."],[152.12,"He has just sort of popped up."],[153.8,"Uh-huh. Uh Saint Silas, Silas is the Greek form uh of the Roman name Sylvanus."],[161.64,"Uh-huh. Um so while there's there's a lot of literature about this, it's generally accepted that uh in 1st and 2 Peter the Sylvanus that Saint Peter refers to, uh who's with him in Rome, is the same person."],[178.04,"Uh it's also uh the same name as Siloan."],[182.2,"Uh-huh. So later saints who are named Saint Siloan are named after him."],[188.12,"Uh-huh. Uh, because there's not a V in Greek."],[191.32,"So the V becomes a U."],[193.24,"And so from Sylvanus you get Silo Siloon."],[194.92,"Um So, um, the the main thing that happened in Acts chapter 15, though, uh, was uh there had to be a decision sort of rendered um by the leaders of the church in Jerusalem."],[213.44,"Uh because what's happened as uh Saints Paul and Barnabas have been going on in these journeys is that not only have a portion of the Jewish communities in these cities been accepting Jesus as the Messiah and becoming Christians, and another there's another segment of the Jewish community that's rejected it."],[235.52,"Uh, but there have also been Gentiles, non Jews, who, while they were connected to the synagogues, and while they knew some things about Judaism, were not actually converts to Judaism, who have heard about Jesus, who have accepted him as the Messiah, and who have also joined the Christian community."],[256.68,"And so this question arose as to what their status was and how they should be brought into the Christian community."],[267.72,"Specifically, do they need to become Jews first?"],[270.92,"Do they need to be circumcised and keep the Jewish food laws and keep the Sabbath regulations, do all the things that are required of Jews first and then become Christians, or could the Gentiles just be. admitted as they are to worshiping and following Christ"],[289.84,"And so this has caused sort of a conflict in some of these communities."],[295.6,"And the danger that St."],[298.56,"Paul and the other apostles want to avert is there being a split in the church where you have sort of Jewish churches and gentile churches."],[307.54,"Which they see as a real problem and something we need to avoid."],[312.9,"And so as we saw St."],[315.06,"James, who was the overseer of the church in Jerusalem, sort of presided at the meeting."],[320.54,"Saint Peter talked about his experience with Cornelius the Centurion, about how when Cornelius had heard the gospel. from Saint Peter and had prayed, he had received the Holy Spirit even before he was baptized"],[336.54,"And so Saint Peter essentially said, Well, look, if he's already received the Holy Spirit, how are we going to make him go back and get circumcised and do all these other things?"],[345.6,"He's already received the Holy Spirit, he's already one of us."],[348.92,"And Saint Paul and St."],[350.84,"Barnabas then talked about the similar things that they had seen with the Gentiles who had come to faith and received the Holy Spirit on their journeys."],[359.32,"And then Saint James sort of rendered a decision on behalf of everyone."],[365.64,"And we talked about how that decision is one that's often misinterpreted."],[370.04,"Uh, because that decision and the letter that was sent out, we have the text of the letter in Acts chapter 15, says that the only things that the Gentiles need to really be concerned about from the law are that they stay away from sexual immorality, food offered to idols, meat of strangled animals, and eating blood."],[393.84,"And those are related because an animal that was strangled would still have the blood in it."],[398.4,"So it's really sort of three things: eating blood, sexual immorality, and food offered to idols, meaning idolatry, participating in idolatrous worship."],[410.64,"And so sometimes that's interpreted, we mentioned last time by modern people as saying, oh, well, as Christians, then we could just sort of ignore, you know, the rest of the Old Testament."],[418.32,"You know, we can just forget about it"],[424.28,"But in actuality, we saw uh last time that in uh Leviticus uh 17 through 23, which is called the Holiness Code, uh, where all those rules, all the food laws and all the regulations are laid out for the people of Israel, there are only three places where it says this rule is not only for Israel, but it's for any strangers, any visitors, any aliens, any travelers."],[453.86,"So when it says don't eat shellfish, it doesn't say you don't eat shellfish, and none of your guests, and none of your right, it doesn't say that, it just says you as a Jew, don't eat shellfish."],[464.62,"But there are three places referring to idolatry, referring to sexual immorality, and referring to eating blood, where it says, not you, and not anyone who's staying with you, and not anyone who's living in the land, and not anyone who's and so what St."],[481.34,"James is doing is reading the Torah, reading Leviticus very literally."],[487.66,"He says, Okay, you know, some of these rules, as they're written, were just given to the Jewish people."],[494.82,"And God nowhere in there said, You need to go out and make sure the Gentiles don't eat shellfish."],[500.64,"He never said that."],[501.76,"He never said, you need to go out and make sure the Gentiles don't work on the Sabbath."],[505.85,"He never said that."],[506.25,"said But there are particular things where he, this is just unacceptable."],[511.99,"Unacceptable. And so St."],[513.99,"James says, well, we're going to treat the church the same way."],[517.21,"These Gentiles who come into the church, we're not going to make them follow the food rules."],[521.21,"We're not going to make them be circumcised."],[522.73,"We're not going to make them follow all of these other rules."],[525.85,"But sexual immorality, eating blood, and idolatry are just unacceptable for Christians."],[533.45,"Why is eating blood so bad?"],[537.53,"This is is one one of of the the this pieces of really good evidence that this is connected to Leviticus."],[543.67,"Because that's that's the only place in the Old Testament where it sort of ranks up there with there are a number of reasons."],[550.07,"One is is it's related to idolatry in a lot of cases"],[556.99,"Um of the the idol worship."],[560.03,"But in the Old Testament, uh blood uh signifies life."],[568.51,"In fact, Leviticus says that the blood of a creature is its life."],[574.19,"And uh so when uh animals were sacrificed according to the rules of the Old Covenant, the blood was drained out, it was taken, it was poured out at the foot of the altar."],[586.67,"So it wouldn't be, it was sort of given back to the ground."],[589.77,"It wasn't used for anything else."],[592.81,"And so uh it's connected to this idea that you know, um let me let me say I'm not arguing for vegetarianism here."],[604.05,"But but when you read the Old Testament carefully, you'll notice that originally God did not give people permission to kill and eat animals."],[612.37,"That only happens after the flood in Genesis, that human beings are given permission to kill and eat animals."],[620.21,"Um, so the Garden of Eden, they weren't killing animals."],[623.58,"The Garden of Eden, you notice how it's phrased, it says, You eat the fruit of the tree."],[627.98,"They weren't even killing plants in order to eat in the Garden of Eden."],[632.86,"You know, and then after the Garden of Eden, they're given permission to, you know, eat whatever plants, but not to go and kill animals for. food"],[640.66,"And so there's this there's this idea that there is something, while it's sometimes necessary, there's something about we as human beings being predatory."],[653.54,"Um, us uh consuming the life of other of God's creatures."],[659.94,"That um, while permissible, is is sort of not ideal."],[665.88,"And I think that's why, for example, we fast from flesh meats for big chunks of the year during Lent and during the other fasts, um, then on Wednesdays and Fridays."],[676.92,"Um, that's why when monasteries fast from flesh meats pretty much all the time."],[684.16,"So it's not impermissible to eat meat, you know, especially on feast days and that kind of thing, celebratory."],[691.36,"But there needs to be sort of an acknowledgement."],[694.16,"And, you know, my relatives who are farmers, you go back a generation or two, had a very different relationship to the animals that they worked with, even the ones they ended up eating."],[708.72,"You know, they had a very different relationship with them than I do to most of the meat I eat that I get packaged, you know, from some factory farm, you know, where the animals are genetically manipulated and you know, everything else, you know, treated who knows how."],[727.12,"So there's a different sort of respect there, you know, where there's sort of an acknowledgement that, you know, yeah, I if I have to go out there and actually kill the animal and clean it and everything before I eat it, I'm gonna have a different respect and appreciation for other living things than when, you know, everything I eat is a patty."],[748.68,"You know, that I get frozen, you know, that doesn't look like an animal."],[753.08,"No, but you believe in that in what?"],[756.44,"You go out go out there and kill an animal."],[759.4,"I think you have a different level of respect for it, yeah."],[762.28,"I think you have a different I'm saying."],[765.8,"Yeah, you do you believe in that?"],[767.64,"Yeah. Yeah, I think you do."],[769.56,"I think you do."],[770.36,"Because I I watched my grandfather kill chick chensick andens and helped him clean and rabbits that kind of thing."],[777.92,"Your family right, yeah."],[779.36,"I mean, you go put food on the table."],[781.68,"Yeah. There it is."],[784.96,"Yeah. Yeah. So that's I mean, that that's the way I was raised."],[791.92,"I'm like you."],[793.44,"I was raised that way."],[794.64,"Yeah. I mean, we killed a hog or something."],[798.32,"Yeah. I mean, that was that was food on the table."],[801.44,"Yeah. Yeah. Um so I think I think that's all related there."],[809.12,"The other interesting thing, of course, is that this prohibition against drinking blood is is super clear here and all through the Old Testament."],[816.8,"And yet if you pause to think about it a second. every time we celebrate the Eucharist, we drink Christ's blood"],[826.2,"And so I think sometimes, you know, like when we read John 6, and Jesus says, you know, unless you eat my flesh and drink my blood, you have no life in you."],[838.04,"And the Jews all start to leave."],[840.2,"You know, we don't appreciate just how shocking that would be."],[843.32,"Not just suggesting that you eat him, but now we're drinking we're drinking blood, let alone people's blood."],[851.8,"You know, this is craziness."],[853.88,"You know, how put the blood in the Buddha?"],[860.2,"A lot of a lot of cultures, a lot of cultures do that."],[862.76,"Say the Germans blood was do that with sausages and stuff too."],[863.72,"So it's not ordered, it's not anymore"],[869.6,"Well, I mean, technically speaking, yeah, it's a it's a violation of this of this rule."],[874.56,"So how did it come into the Catholicism because the Akkadians are really uh Catholics?"],[879.84,"I I I think part of it may have just been you know, poverty."],[884.48,"You had to eat it."],[885.44,"You gotta eat what you gotta eat, yeah, you know, sometimes."],[889.04,"And if that's what you've got, that's what you've got to do."],[892.32,"You know. Um but yeah, so we we we don't appreciate just how shocking that idea was to Jewish people that we would be drinking Christ's blood."],[902.8,"You know, this is bizarre."],[904.4,"But if we accept that the blood of something is its life, then what are we doing?"],[912.14,"We're receiving Christ's life."],[915.34,"Right within ourselves."],[918.19,"And so part of making blood sort of sacred and taboo in the Old Testament and the Old Covenant is to help reveal to us, you know, in the fulfillment of sacrifice."],[935.09,"In the Eucharist that we celebrate when we eat Christ's body and drink his blood, so that we understand it more fully."],[943.33,"Okay You know."],[945.53,"So that's very helpful."],[949.09,"Yeah, that was not the short version, but I hope I covered the ground."],[955.21,"Okay. So, unless there are any other questions or comments, we'll go ahead and get started here finally in uh Acts the Apostles, chapter 16."],[965.29,"Okay. So uh Saints uh Paul and Silas are who we're following now."],[972.09,"And they went through, they left Antioch and went through Syria and Cilicia."],[978.25,"And uh now, as we begin in chapter 16, they're going back to the churches that they had founded in some of the cities in Asia Minor and what's now Turkey."],[989.85,"So chapter 16, verse 1."],[991.61,"Then he came to Derby and Lystra, and behold, a certain disciple was there named Timothy. the son of a certain Jewish woman who believed, but his father was Greek"],[1003.97,"So Timothy, it was going to be Saint Timothy, who the two letters, first and second Timothy, are written to."],[1013.01,"Saint Timothy is the first second generation Christian we run into."],[1018.93,"Second generation Christian."],[1021.41,"His mother's a Christian."],[1023.33,"And raised him as a Christian."],[1025.22,"Everybody else so far has been somebody who became a Christian as an adult."],[1028.82,"St. Timothy here is a young man."],[1031.22,"His mother is a Jewish woman who accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[1034.96,"Who's a believer."],[1037.1,"But his father, but it says his father was Greek."],[1040.98,"You notice it doesn't say anything about believing, meaning he's just a Gentile."],[1046.6,"He's just a Gentile."],[1050.28,"He, meaning Timothy, was well spoken of by the brethren who were at Lystra and Iconium."],[1056.36,"Remember, these are churches that St."],[1057.88,"Paul founded on their last journey."],[1061.32,"Paul wanted to have him go on with him, and he took him and circumcised him because of the Jews who were in that region."],[1068.12,"For they all knew that his father was Greek."],[1072.76,"Now, this seems like a contradiction of what we just read in Acts 15."],[1077.4,"We had this big hubbub."],[1079.24,"Remember it? It was St."],[1080.36,"Paul was just adamant."],[1082.6,"Don't need to be circumcised"],[1082.92,"Don't need to be circumcised."],[1084.6,"Don't need to be circumcised."],[1085.56,"And so now he finds this young man"],[1090.72,"He wants to start teaching this young man to be a leader of the church."],[1095.04,"And so he goes and has him circumcised."],[1098.96,"So this seems to be a contradiction."],[1101.04,"Seems to be."],[1103.2,"But a couple of things."],[1105.28,"Remember that St."],[1106.24,"Paul was adamant that Gentiles who became Christians not be circumcised."],[1111.84,"So question one is Is Saint Timothy a Gentile?"],[1118.32,"His mother, his mother is Jewish."],[1121.28,"Oh, that's true."],[1122.4,"And being Jewish goes in the. world"],[1124.4,"Since his mother is Jewish, he's Jewish."],[1128.34,"So, yes, his father. is a Gentile and not a believer in anything, as far as we know, probably a pagan"],[1135.51,"But he is."],[1137.29,"Saint Paul is looking at, remember, what his strategy is because he goes to these cities, he goes to the synagogue first."],[1145.5,"Then preaches the gospel there."],[1147.66,"And then sort of moves out from there."],[1150.86,"Well, if he's going to be going into the synagogue, Saint Timothy is from the perspective of those Jewish communities he's going to be going to, he's not a Gentile."],[1161.02,"He's an uncircumcised Jew."],[1163.74,"And so, what are they going to say about Saint Paul?"],[1167.66,"They're going to say, not only does he not want the Gentiles to be circumcised, he's going around telling Jews not to circumcise their children."],[1178.1,"So Timothy probably wasn't circumcised because his father circumcised objective."],[1183.62,"Probably because of his father, wouldn't allow it."],[1186.47,"And so Saint Paul, and that's not something Saint Paul is doing."],[1191.91,"He's not going around to Jewish people and telling them not to, you know, hey, stop following the laws and customs of our people."],[1199.11,"He's not doing that."],[1201.75,"And so he doesn't want that accusation to be brought."],[1204.55,"Why doesn't he want that accusation to be brought?"],[1206.87,"Well, because what's his goal?"],[1210.39,"He's to bring the Jews in into the church as well."],[1213.77,"To bring these people to Christ."],[1215.22,"And so if him having this young Jewish man who isn't circumcised with him is going to be a stumbling block, it's going to cause people not to listen to him."],[1224.54,"It's going to cause people to not hear the gospel or not accept it."],[1228.11,"Then it's better for him to just be circumcised than to potentially alienate these people."],[1235.23,"And one of his letters, St."],[1236.35,"Paul is going to say that he's, you know, all things to all people, so that he might by all means save some."],[1243.28,"So this is something about St."],[1246.64,"Paul. St. Paul is always very clear about his goal."],[1250.56,"His goal is to bring the whole world to Christ."],[1254.24,"Everybody. And so if he's trying to bring Gentiles to Christ, and they have to go through this whole process of becoming Jewish."],[1262.64,"That's going to be an obstacle."],[1264.57,"That's going to be a stumbling block."],[1265.85,"That's going to stop them."],[1266.73,"That's going to impede them."],[1267.85,"So he doesn't want to do that."],[1270.14,"But if St."],[1271.02,"Timothy not being circumcised is going to put a stumbling block and an obstacle in the path of these Jewish communities coming to believe in Jesus, he's not going to do that either."],[1281.2,"So that's his goal."],[1282.4,"His goal is always, you know, any unnecessary obstacle."],[1286.74,"There are plenty of obstacles already."],[1288.98,"He's already showing up and saying, oh, hey, everybody, the Messiah already came, and guess what?"],[1292.98,"The Romans crucified him."],[1295.92,"He's already telling them, oh, by the way, every time we gather, we eat his flesh and we drink his blood."],[1302.01,"He's already got a lot of stuff to tell them that they're going to have problems with."],[1308.97,"He doesn't want to add one thing to that."],[1312.57,"He doesn't want to add any ethnic stuff to that."],[1314.97,"He doesn't want to add any, you know, religious stuff to that."],[1319.61,"It's enough as it is, you know, the gospel that he's preaching."],[1323.45,"He wants to add nothing else."],[1325.77,"And so that is that is what St."],[1328.73,"Paul is consistent about."],[1331.31,"And so that's why he goes here and he has St."],[1334.27,"Timothy circumcised, even after all of that that we just went through."],[1338.99,"And as they went through the cities, they delivered to them. the decrees to keep, which were determined by the apostles and elders at Jerusalem"],[1349.03,"So the churches were strengthened in the faith and increased in number daily."],[1352.87,"So they bring with them the letter from St."],[1355.35,"James that he sent out to all the churches."],[1357.67,"The decrees, these are the things, idolatry, sexual immorality, blood that you need to refrain from."],[1365.51,"And so they're going to place strengthening the churches, and these churches that they've planted are growing continuously in the communities."],[1373.59,"Now when they had gone through Phry Phrygia and the region of Galatia, they were forbidden by the Holy Spirit to preach the word in Asia."],[1382.19,"After they're still in Asia."],[1385.03,"Well, Asia Minor."],[1386.15,"This is Asia proper."],[1387.35,"Oh, okay. So they've gone through Galatia."],[1388.95,"Galatia is more to the north"],[1392.19,"So this is sort of the middle of what's now Turkey, and they've gone from south to north."],[1397.15,"And they were presumably going to go further up into Asia was their original plan."],[1403.87,"But uh they're not permitted to."],[1407.55,"After they had come to Misra, they tried to go into Bithynia, but the Spirit did not permit them."],[1414.67,"So passing by Mysia, they came down to Troaz, and a vision appeared to Paul in the night."],[1420.67,"A man of Macedonia stood and pleaded with him, saying, Come over to Macedonia and help us."],[1426.43,"After he had seen the vision, immediately, we sought to go to Macedonia, concluding that the Lord had called us to preach the gospel to them."],[1435.51,"Do you notice what happened in there?"],[1440.07,"There was a change in pronoun."],[1453.35,"Now, after he had seen the vision, immediately we sought to go to Macedonia, concluding that the Lord had called us to preach the gospel to them."],[1461.59,"Oh, okay. He just spoke to me."],[1463.99,"He said, This is the dream I had."],[1465.75,"Well, no, no, St."],[1466.79,"Paul had the dream."],[1468.63,"But what St."],[1470.23,"Luke let slip is that St."],[1471.51,"Luke is now there too."],[1473.03,"Oh, I think."],[1474.15,"St. Luke is now part of this group."],[1476.15,"So we've got St."],[1476.95,"Paul, St. Silas, St."],[1478.31,"Timothy. Okay Now St."],[1479.79,"Luke is there too."],[1481.79,"Okay. Uh-huh. Yeah, because I thought even when you pointed out the we that it referred to to Silas."],[1488.75,"No, we is it's first person all of a sudden."],[1492.27,"It was he."],[1493.33,"After he, St."],[1494.37,"Paul had the dream."],[1495.7,"Then we decided to go to St."],[1499.38,"Luke has joined them here."],[1502.26,"So the the story here is that they originally had one plan to go deeper into Asia after they had gone back through the churches they had already planted."],[1514.02,"But that by the Holy Spirit's prompting and then by this dream that St."],[1519.94,"Paul has that he should go to Macedonia, they change their plans and go into Macedonia instead, which is the area to the immediate north of Greece."],[1531.58,"So they're veering to the west."],[1534.86,"Now I talked about Saint Luke, I know, in the uh introduction way back at the beginning of the book."],[1543.26,"Um, but uh what we know about uh Saint Luke is that uh he was a um physician, um, and as we mentioned in the introduction, that was a little different in the first century than doctors today, you know, um, but not completely dissimilar."],[1567.32,"I mean, you, he wasn't sort of a weird mystic or something who was know, throwing chicken feathers at him or something."],[1574.57,"I mean, this was they they they were doing science as they understood it at that time."],[1578.65,"It's just they didn't have near as much understanding as we have now."],[1582.49,"But so they were trying to understand the human body and disease and different ways of relieving pain and ailments."],[1589.93,"And so this means that St."],[1591.77,"Luke was a very uh wealthy person."],[1596.01,"Um he was uh a very well-educated person."],[1601.13,"He would have been one of the most educated people in that in that society."],[1607.93,"Um there were in the Roman world at this time, I mean, there were medical. textbooks that we still have, Galen and others"],[1617.57,"And some of it is some of it is very good and still useful today, and some of it is pretty crazy."],[1623.57,"From our perspective now."],[1625.19,"But they were making an effort."],[1626.73,"And so this was a legitimate field of learning."],[1630.01,"This is someone that something that someone needs to learn and study."],[1633.05,"That explains to us how St."],[1635.53,"Luke could have written, both his gospel and the book of Acts."],[1640.73,"As we mentioned, they're they're written in a Greek style that's based on Thucydides and other classical historians."],[1648.09,"So if you were educated enough to be a physician, you would have read widely, you know, it's sort of history and and literature and poetry and that kind of thing. from the Roman world"],[1661.01,"Traditionally, we understand that he was from Antioch, and that that's where he would have become a Christian, and where he would have met Saint Paul."],[1671.01,"We read how St."],[1671.89,"Paul spent several years preaching and teaching in Antioch."],[1677.09,"There's a lot of debate as to St."],[1680.05,"Luke's ethnic background, whether he was Jewish or not."],[1685.97,"There are a number of things in in uh his gospel in particular regarding geography and that kind of thing in Judea um and the way he transliterates Hebrew words and that kind of thing that people used to conclude that he probably was not Jewish."],[1706.09,"Um but there are also some what are called Semiticisms uh in his writing meaning there they're sort of ways of structuring a sentence and ways of using grammar and that kind of thing that aren't really proper Greek but that are proper to Semitic languages like Aramaic and and Syriac and Arabic and and that type of language other than Hebrew."],[1732.97,"So there's currently most scholars think that that Saint Luke was was ethnically from another Semitic people group."],[1744.61,"So that he was probably Syriac and living in Antioch and had become had become a Christian."],[1751.97,"So he was a Syrian Christian, essentially, ethnically Syrian."],[1765.41,"I was about to say something about that."],[1766.61,"Yeah. And two of them still exist."],[1770.37,"One is on Mount Athos, and I don't remember where the other one is."],[1774.29,"It might be."],[1775.09,"It might be in Rome."],[1777.01,"And they have pretty good cases beyond just the church says so."],[1781.85,"that th that they're authentic because Because, for example, they're painted on wax tablets, uh which is how portraits were painted in the first century. and not for very long after that"],[1793.77,"So uh that that makes it very likely that these at least date to that of course you could never prove that a particular person is the one who painted it, but yeah."],[1806.17,"But um but uh there's very good evidence it goes back to that period of uh history."],[1815.37,"And so that combined with the tradition."],[1818.41,"It's sort of like how when they they did the tissue typing on the bones under Saint Peter's Basilica in Rome and discovered that it was a Jewish man from the first century AD."],[1827.77,"It's like, well, you can't prove it's Saint Peter, but I mean, how many Jewish men from the first century could it be?"],[1835.45,"You know. So there's very good evidence for that"],[1843.49,"So this passage here marks the point where St."],[1846.93,"Luke is accompanying them."],[1847.97,"And so now we have this sort of larger group of traveling companions who are traveling and working together to spread the gospel."],[1858.21,"Okay. Therefore, sailing from Troaz, we ran a straight course to Samothrace, and the next day came to Neapolis, and from there to Philippi, which is the foremost city of that part of Macedonia, a colony."],[1875.89,"And we were staying in that city for some days."],[1878.45,"Okay, notice it's all wee wee wee now."],[1880.65,"It's going to stay that way."],[1883.61,"So they travel from the northern part of Asia Minor across to Macedonia, which is up above Greece."],[1893.05,"And uh Philippi, of course, there are lots of Philippis."],[1900.01,"We talked before about how there were several Antiochs and a whole bunch of Alexandrias."],[1905.13,"Well, there's a whole bunch of Philippians for the same reason, because of course Alexander the Great's father, who was the king of Macedonia, was Philip of Macedon."],[1914.09,"So there's a bunch of Philippias too."],[1917.85,"It's a colony."],[1918.97,"Does that mean it's a Roman settlement?"],[1921.05,"It's a Roman colony, this one."],[1922.57,"Okay But they named it in honor of Philip anyway."],[1926.05,"Yeah Because it's in Macedonia."],[1929.43,"And so this is this is a port city on the uh."],[1938.31,"On the eastern side."],[1939.43,"And so this is because it's a port city, that's why it's become such a forebo city, even though it's a colony, even though it's a recent city."],[1949.11,"Um the one thing the Romans were really good at was building roads and trade routes."],[1955.69,"And keeping the economy moving."],[1957.85,"Um so any port city very quickly became an important hub."],[1969.96,"Yeah, we have a good path to do it"],[1970.77,"If we have to come back and destroy you, we have a nice neat road."],[1976.13,"And also remember, these cut the colony cities were mostly military veterans."],[1981.97,"Okay. Yeah. Yeah."],[1983.41,"They were military."],[1986.13,"Yeah. After you'd served a certain period in the military and earned your citizenship, you were granted a piece of land."],[1993.33,"And the robots would grant that piece of land out at the edge of their territory to set up these new colony cities."],[1999.41,"So groups of veterans would retire, set up this city, and then their job was to sort of bring the Roman way of life to you know this new area."],[2008.93,"That's what they need to harbor."],[2010.27,"And integrate it into the empire."],[2012.67,"And so in this case, because it's a port, they're doing a pretty good job."],[2016.15,"Because this is one of the similarities between the Roman Empire and the United States, is that uh when our money and our culture gets into a place, it tends to sort of transform it into sort of a mirror image of us."],[2031.99,"You know, so the same kind of thing happened with Rome then, you know."],[2034.95,"So this may have been a barbarous region before where they had their own culture, whatever that was, but as soon as Rome shows up with a lot of money and a lot of trade, and it sort of all becomes Rome."],[2046.53,"I think of Thessalonica as the big port in that area."],[2050.37,"Is this far from there or not?"],[2053.17,"Well, you have to remember, at this time in history, see, we think of port cities as. destination cities"],[2062.49,"Because when someone sails from London to New York, it's a straight shot across the Atlantic Ocean."],[2069.69,"They weren't sailing straight across the Mediterranean or even the Aegean."],[2074.17,"They sailed along the coast."],[2076.57,"And so you had to put in sort of at various points all the way along the route."],[2084.25,"And so port became a travel stop."],[2088.25,"You know, where commerce would be done, where the sailors would go and do what sailors do when they make port, you know."],[2096.09,"And so um, there were a lot more ports than than now because of that, because they had to make all these way stops along. the way"],[2105.41,"So Thessalonica may have been their final destination, or Ephesus might have been their final destination, or their original port of call, but they would have to stop many places along the way, and all of those places got sort of like Blythe California."],[2125.33,"There's a there's a city, there's a city in California called Blythe, which is such a lovely name for a town."],[2133.41,"It's in the middle of the desert."],[2136.13,"There's nothing there, but it just happens to be the halfway point between Los Angeles and Phoenix."],[2142.53,"And so first a gas station popped up, and then another gas station popped up, and then a bigger gas station popped up, and then a casino popped up, and then and so over the course of about 20 years, Blythe became this huge sort of travel fun stop for people traveling in between the two."],[2160.49,"So it's a similar kind of dynamic with some of these new port cities."],[2167.77,"And so all these things grow up around it."],[2174.41,"Okay, so this is this is the city where they choose to begin in Macedonia."],[2179.69,"And so they stop there first and they stay there for some time."],[2184.65,"And on the Sabbath day, we went out of the city to the riverside where prayer was customarily made."],[2191.01,"And we sat down and spoke to the women who met there."],[2196.29,"So this is a place that doesn't seem to have much of an organized synagogue."],[2202.71,"Which makes sense if this is a Roman colony."],[2205.83,"Okay. There probably weren't any Jewish people in the area, or many."],[2210.73,"We're going to see there's a few."],[2212.41,"But there probably weren't many Jewish people in the area originally."],[2216.25,"The people who came and settled it were all Romans, who, of course, weren't Jewish."],[2221.21,"So there's only this small Jewish community there."],[2223.77,"And so when they gather on the Sabbath day to pray, they just go down to the riverside near town and pray there."],[2230.17,"And it seems to be a group that was primarily consisted of women."],[2236.89,"Now a certain woman named Lydia heard us."],[2238.81,"She was a seller of purple from the city of Thyatira who worshipped God"],[2244.45,"The Lord opened her heart to heed the things spoken by Paul."],[2249.17,"That's purple. This is purple dye."],[2253.25,"Purple dye for purple clothes."],[2257.09,"It comes from a shellfish."],[2258.37,"There's these tiny shells from a certain species of shellfish that they would crush up to get small quantities of purple dye."],[2266.85,"So it's very expensive."],[2268.45,"And therefore very prized and very showy."],[2271.41,"If you had this is why emperors wore purple."],[2275.01,"So if you had a whole garment that was all purple, that meant you were very rich."],[2279.45,"The Mardi Gras colors being purple and gold, there were regal colors."],[2283.85,"Royalty. And so uh what this is saying, this tells us first of all why this Jewish woman had come to this city."],[2292.52,"It's this poor city."],[2293.48,"This is a place where she can ply her trade."],[2296.92,"Sell the purple dyes, sell the purple garments."],[2301.4,"Yeah. Yeah. So this is a good place for her to do business."],[2305.24,"This tells us sort of why she's here."],[2307.0,"Also tells us she would have been a woman of some means."],[2310.69,"But she'd probably be a widow since uh she's running the business now that her husband."],[2315.49,"Well, we don't know."],[2316.93,"We don't know."],[2317.73,"It would be it would be very difficult, but not impossible in Roman society for a woman to get into that kind of business on her own."],[2327.57,"So I mean we are told, you know, so we don't know"],[2333.21,"She may have just been a particularly remarkable woman who managed to do that, you know."],[2337.93,"Um or she may be uh a widow, or her father, she may have not been married, and her father, her parents might have been in that business, and she sort of took it over after them."],[2358.89,"And when she and her household were baptized, she begged us, saying, If you have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come to my house and stay."],[2367.69,"So she persuaded us."],[2369.85,"So household, um, we have to remember, we tend to think of we hear household and we think, oh, well, she must have had kids."],[2381.11,"So this was this must be your kids."],[2383.59,"But we have to remember that the Roman family unit was much larger than what we consider a family unit."],[2390.79,"And not only included parents, brothers, sisters, aunts, uncles, cousins, but household servants, slaves, everybody."],[2400.98,"Everybody who household met, you know, your holding, your landholdings, everyone who lived there, everyone who worked there, probably included everyone who worked for her in her business."],[2411.04,"So that's so when it says household, we don't know per se how many individuals this was or how exactly they were related to her."],[2415.12,"Okay But what's important here is that when she comes to faith, her whole household is baptized."],[2431.08,"That seems to happen a lot."],[2432.76,"Yeah, we're gonna see more of that."],[2435.22,"But her whole her whole household is baptized."],[2438.18,"Um so we have to remember we live in a very individualistic age in our society, and this is why many of our Protestant friends have a problem with babies being baptized."],[2453.94,"Because the baby can't sort of make a profession of faith."],[2457.92,"The baby can't make its own decision, the baby. can't accept"],[2463.48,"And that's what we think of people primarily as individuals."],[2467.96,"At this time in history, that's not how people were thought of."],[2474.92,"The word persona in Latin, that we get the word person from meant literally face."],[2484.78,"Refer to your public face."],[2487.1,"That's who you were."],[2489.02,"So when they're thinking at that time, who you were was defined by your relationships to other people."],[2497.63,"How many people have we seen whose name is Bar something?"],[2501.83,"Son of son of somebody."],[2503.67,"What defines who you are is this is your father, and this is your relationship to him"],[2510.81,"Right. Romans had four names."],[2515.93,"Only one of those was a personal name."],[2519.53,"It was their father's name, their grandfather's name, their family name, and then a given name."],[2526.25,"And most of the names that we know people by in history are not their given names."],[2534.01,"So, for example, Julius Caesar's given name was Gaius."],[2538.01,"Oh. Huh Cicero's given name was Marcus."],[2544.73,"Those are family names."],[2546.35,"We remember them by their family names."],[2547.15,"by their family. Remember the individual name"],[2551.59,"Yeah, the Julian clan was the Julio Claudian dynasty, yeah."],[2559.83,"So yeah, we we we need to not think look read this through our modern sort of cultural eyes."],[2570.85,"And so this is this is a time when families and social units made decisions together."],[2579.47,"Made decisions together."],[2581.55,"Now we just saw, for example, Saint Timothy, but what happened with Saint Timothy was a Jewish woman married a Gentile man."],[2591.07,"If that whole family was going to convert to something, they would have done it as a unit."],[2597.89,"So you get these hodgepodges."],[2599.75,"Where people are in different states when they marry."],[2602.63,"That would have been much more of a tension than it would be today."],[2606.31,"Yeah. And that tension really only occurred with like Judaism."],[2609.59,"Because what would normally happen when two pagans got married, you'd have a pagan from one place who worships certain gods, and you'd have a pagan from another place who worships certain other family gods and that kind of thing."],[2619.51,"And when they got married, they just sort of smush them all together."],[2623.43,"You know, they'd have household gods and they'd have a whole bunch."],[2626.63,"You know, they would just sort of smush everything together"],[2629.93,"Now that doesn't work with a Jewish person."],[2632.35,"Because the Jewish person isn't going to worship their spouse's gods."],[2633.15,"isn't going to worship. And the spouse necessarily the Jewish god"],[2640.28,"And it would almost look like if it was your entire family in a way."],[2643.54,"And we're going to see people who people who become Christians not as part of their family, but as individuals, are seen to be leaving their family."],[2655.7,"If you decide I'm not going to worship our family's gods anymore, I'm only going to worship Jesus Christ."],[2661.44,"You're out of the family."],[2663.2,"You're no longer part of that family."],[2664.88,"You're no longer part of that social unit."],[2666.88,"You've rejected it."],[2668.32,"Well, it's still a big deal if a Jewish person marries a Gentile, especially a woman marrying the Gentile."],[2675.28,"Yeah. And it's not favored by any means."],[2677.12,"'s Sometimes tolerated, but so that we need to understand that dynamic."],[2680.2,"Yeah. And so that's why we're gonna see we're gonna see those two dynamics."],[2689.96,"Either we're gonna see a whole household becoming Christian as a unit."],[2695.02,"As a family unit."],[2696.46,"Or we're gonna see somebody from that household becoming a Christian and being cast out of that household."],[2706.22,"Those are the dynamics at play."],[2708.78,"And so when a whole household becomes Christian, that means the whole household."],[2713.1,"In a Roman home, at this time in history, like they uh she invites Lydia invites them to stay in her home."],[2721.62,"In a Roman home, they had a hearth in the center."],[2728.28,"Because we have to remember they didn't have indoor heating, or or running hot water or anything."],[2733.03,"So you had a hearth that you kept burning, you know, in the center of the home."],[2737.75,"And uh that's why Vesta, the goddess of the hearth, was so important in Roman religion."],[2744.95,"Um then at that hearth, that was the place where the household gods would be."],[2751.67,"There would be idols or images of the household gods there at the hearth."],[2757.75,"And when the family worshiped together, when the family gathered to pray and to worship. together for religious observances, they'd gather at that hearth"],[2770.43,"They'd gather with those those household gods."],[2775.76,"And those gods were viewed as being part of the family and part of the community with them."],[2781.44,"This was true of cities as a whole as well."],[2784.24,"The gods of the city were part of the community of the city."],[2789.36,"And so we're going to see this is going to be part of what generates the persecution of Christians by the Romans."],[2794.96,"Is that when the Christians say, I'm not going to worship this city's gods anymore, I know I live in Athens, but I'm not worshiping Athena anymore."],[2802.18,"Because I think Athena is a demon now."],[2805.4,"They're gonna-that's this is why they're going to be persecuted, because they're going to say, Look, you're going to make her angry."],[2809.2,"She's the one who brings prosperity and success and wealth to our city"],[2814.12,"Why are you spitting in her face like this by refusing to worship her?"],[2818.68,"You're gonna bring her anger down on all of us."],[2821.56,"So we need to get rid of you."],[2824.29,"You get rid of you, and that's what's gonna bring this persecution."],[2827.81,"And what we're gonna see happening already within the pages, when we get to 1 Corinthians, I'll talk about this more."],[2833.97,"Already within the pages of Scripture, the Gentiles who convert are removing those household gods and household idols and replacing them with iconography."],[2844.77,"They're replacing them with images of Christ and his mother and of the saints."],[2850.55,"And they're gathering there to pray and worship."],[2852.87,"And this is where the idea of a prayer corner or a home altar of the Orthodox Church of having a place in your home that's set apart where you have icons, where you have holy water, where you have poor things, where you go to as a family to gather to pray, that's where this comes from."],[2871.31,"It goes all the way back to the early church."],[2876.51,"This tradition of having this place."],[2882.27,"That includes everybody."],[2883.47,"If there were any kids, that includes the kids."],[2886.01,"Everybody made this decision together."],[2888.75,"It wasn't against anybody's will."],[2891.09,"They didn't force somebody, you know."],[2893.41,"You you either you get baptized or I'm gonna fire you from my purple selling business."],[2897.42,"There was none of that."],[2898.99,"But the there also would not have been sort of, okay, let's take a vote, guys."],[2903.23,"Do we wanna be Christians?"],[2905.55,"Do we want to be Jewish?"],[2906.99,"Do we want to be uh counterways?"],[2908.83,"Okay, everybody write out a piece of paper and we'll put it in a hat."],[2911.41,"There was none of that."],[2912.47,"Decisions were made, you know, as a group."],[2915.59,"And so Lydia becomes this very important figure now in Philippi."],[2920.65,"Sort of her home, her household now sort of becomes home base for the church, you know, that's now forming in Philippi."],[2932.17,"One one more note on that."],[2934.17,"Um I know I've said this before, but maybe not recently."],[2938.09,"But an inordinate number of the early. converts to Christianity were women and slaves"],[2945.01,"Um because for a very simple reason."],[2948.85,"Christianity was the only religion that existed at this time in history that said that people who were slaves, women, children, were of equal value to other human beings."],[2966.05,"Christianity was the only religion that said every human being is created in the image of God and is equal in God's sight."],[2972.17,"Now you might say, well, Judaism says that too."],[2974.61,"Well, yeah, unless you're a Gentile."],[2979.49,"So at this point in history, Christianity is the only religion that's teaching that."],[2984.53,"And we underestimate how powerful that is because we take it for granted"],[2991.77,"You know. But having someone who's been a slave for three generations and coming them into saying, you know what, the God who created the universe loves you, and he loves you just as much, and you're just as important to him as Caesar is."],[3004.73,"In fact, if you follow Jesus Christ, you're gonna come out a lot better in this than Caesar will."],[3010.35,"Because he's gonna have to give an account for all the things he's done."],[3013.15,"How powerful that is."],[3015.55,"You know, when that's when that's preached and that's proclaimed and that's believed."],[3019.55,"Well, many religions at this time and and in some parts of the world still regard people who experience misfortune as. um cursed by God, and you ought to curse them too"],[3035.11,"You ought to stomp on them a little bit more just to show that you agree with God."],[3038.79,"You've got the caste system in India and you've got the thank you."],[3042.63,"Yeah, and and Rome was not that much different."],[3044.87,"I mean, remember the the vast majority of the population of the Roman Empire was legally known persona, non persons."],[3055.35,"They couldn't bring it if they unless you were a Roman citizen, you had no rights."],[3060.39,"So if you were a slave and the Jews who weren't Roman citizens were lower than slaves."],[3067.31,"You know, this is why they could just they would just go could just go and crucify Jesus with no real charges."],[3074.27,"Pilate, as we mentioned, we were going through the Gospel of John, was worried there was going to be a riot at the Passover about 20 years before Jesus was crucified."],[3082.91,"So he just crucified 150 random Jews along the road."],[3086.91,"Just grabbed them off the street."],[3089.87,"So that the people coming into the city would see them dying and know better than to cause trouble."],[3095.31,"But the Romans didn't consider them human, they considered them like you know animals that were walking around on their land."],[3102.27,"You know. When Jesus, we have that phrase now, going the extra mile."],[3108.11,"You know, where Jesus came and said, if someone compels you to go one mile, go two."],[3113.39,"He was talking about the fact that Roman soldiers could just walk up to a Jewish person, take the pack off their back that they were tired of carrying, and say, hey, you carry this for me for the next mile."],[3127.19,"And if you said no, they could kill you."],[3130.15,"You know, there was no investigation, there was nothing."],[3133.92,"So we, you know, we we don't understand the, you know, thank God we haven't had to, you know, experience that kind of thing firsthand."],[3143.36,"But that I mean society was brutal at that point."],[3146.4,"And they looked at, they didn't think everyone was equally human."],[3150.08,"They didn't think all human beings were equally valuable."],[3153.76,"You know. And if you were a victim, well, that means you're pathetic and you deserve it."],[3160.1,"The theyy had had no no, you know quality."],[3161.62,"you know Paul comes and preaches this to people, to women, who have been marginalized by the society completely, and to people who are slaves, and to people who are poor, and to Jews who are non persons who are being abused daily by the Romans and says to them, you know, God loves you."],[3164.22,"And so when St."],[3182.24,"Nobody thought Zeus loved them."],[3185.12,"No. Nobody was singing, What a Friend We Have in Zeus."],[3189.17,"Like Zeus was angry and threw lightning bolts, and you tried to keep it pacified so he wouldn't smite you."],[3195.73,"You know. But he didn't love you."],[3198.53,"Unless you were a beautiful woman."],[3200.37,"Well, yeah. Well, even then, I don't know if that's love."],[3202.85,"Yeah, that's true."],[3203.89,"He'd use you for his own ends, you know."],[3205.81,"But there was there no was no, you know, love there."],[3208.89,"If you're the emperor, he might know who you were."],[3212.97,"But he didn't love the emperor even."],[3216.46,"But the creator of the earth loves you."],[3219.42,"Cares about it."],[3220.46,"Cares about what's happening to you."],[3222.22,"Cares about what you've had to go through today."],[3225.66,"You know, and the indignities heaped up."],[3227.58,"That's a powerful thing."],[3229.02,"And so that's that's a big force behind why so many people, and especially so many women, so many slaves, so many of the peasants and the underclass who are Roman citizens, convert to Christianity in droves."],[3245.66,"Because they're experiencing the love of God for the first time when this is proclaimed to them."],[3252.02,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3259.38,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3264.74,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.16,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Paul and Barnabas part ways","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Acts 16</b>; earlier episodes (incl. intro to Acts) already up online</li><li class=\"d0\">Apology in advance for <b>coughing</b> - \"if I could stop, I would\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Where we left off: <b>first missionary journey</b> of Paul and Barnabas</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Cyprus</b>, then several cities in <b>Asia Minor</b> (now Turkey)</li><li class=\"d1\">preached, <b>started churches</b> in those cities</li><li class=\"d0\">Preparing a second journey → <b>falling out</b>, the two separate</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Paul</b> takes <b>Silas</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Barnabas</b> takes <b>John Mark</b> (= St. Mark of the gospel)</li><li class=\"d0\">Barnabas and Mark go back to <b>Cyprus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Barnabas <b>from Cyprus</b>, part of the Jewish community there</li><li class=\"d1\">going to bring the gospel to <b>his own Jewish community</b></li></ul>"},{"t":153.8,"lv":3,"h":"Silas = Sylvanus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Silas</b> = Greek form of Roman name <b>Sylvanus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Generally accepted: same <b>Sylvanus</b> with Peter in Rome in <b>1 and 2 Peter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same name as <b>Siloan</b> → later saints named <b>Silouan</b> after him</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>V</b> in Greek - the V becomes a U: Sylvanus → <b>Silouan</b></li></ul>"},{"t":194.92,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 15 recap: the Jerusalem decision","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Main thing in <b>Acts 15</b>: decision by leaders of the church in <b>Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Background: on the journeys, some Jews accept <b>Jesus as Messiah</b>, others reject</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>Gentiles</b> - tied to synagogues, knew Judaism, not converts - accept Jesus</li><li class=\"d0\">Question: <b>do they have to become Jews first?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">circumcision, <b>food laws</b>, <b>Sabbath</b> rules, then Christian?</li><li class=\"d1\">or admitted <b>as they are</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Danger Paul and apostles want to avert: <b>split</b> - Jewish churches vs Gentile churches</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. James</b> (overseer in Jerusalem) presides</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Peter</b>: <b>Cornelius</b> received the <b>Holy Spirit</b> before baptism → \"already one of us\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Paul and Barnabas</b>: same with Gentiles on their journeys</li><li class=\"d1\">James renders decision for everyone</li><li class=\"d0\">Letter (text in Acts 15): only keep from <b>sexual immorality, food offered to idols, strangled meat, blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">strangled animal still has blood → really <b>three things</b>: blood, sexual immorality, idolatry</li></ul>"},{"t":410.64,"lv":3,"h":"Not \"ignore the Old Testament\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern misreading: Christians can <b>ignore the rest of the OT</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Actually: <b>Leviticus 17-23 = Holiness Code</b> - food laws and regulations for Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>three places</b> say rule applies also to <b>strangers, aliens, travelers, anyone in the land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shellfish: says only \"<b>you</b> as a Jew\" - not your guests</li><li class=\"d0\">The three: <b>idolatry</b>, <b>sexual immorality</b>, <b>eating blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">James reads <b>Leviticus very literally</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God never said make Gentiles avoid shellfish or keep the <b>Sabbath</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but these three = \"just <b>unacceptable</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Church treated the same way</li><li class=\"d1\">no food rules, no circumcision for Gentiles</li><li class=\"d1\">but sexual immorality, blood, idolatry <b>unacceptable for Christians</b></li></ul>"},{"t":533.45,"lv":3,"h":"Why is eating blood so bad?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Good evidence the decision is tied to <b>Leviticus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reasons for the blood ban</li><li class=\"d1\">related to <b>idolatry</b> in many cases - idol worship</li><li class=\"d1\">OT: <b>blood = life</b>; Leviticus: \"the blood of a creature is its <b>life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sacrifice rules: blood <b>drained</b>, poured out at foot of the <b>altar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">given back to the ground, <b>not used for anything else</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Original <b>Eden</b>: no permission to kill and eat animals</li><li class=\"d1\">\"eat the <b>fruit</b> of the tree\" - not even killing plants</li><li class=\"d1\">after Eden: plants, still not animals</li><li class=\"d1\">permission only <b>after the flood</b> (Genesis)</li><li class=\"d0\">Hence something about human beings being <b>predatory</b> - consuming the life of God's creatures</li><li class=\"d1\">\"while permissible, sort of <b>not ideal</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":665.88,"lv":2,"h":"Fasting, farmers, respect for animals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why we <b>fast from flesh meats</b> - Lent, other fasts, Wednesdays and Fridays</li><li class=\"d1\">monasteries fast from flesh meats nearly all the time</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not arguing for <b>vegetarianism</b>\"; meat fine on <b>feast days</b>, celebration</li><li class=\"d0\">But needs an <b>acknowledgement</b></li><li class=\"d1\">relatives who were <b>farmers</b> - different relationship to animals they ate</li><li class=\"d1\">vs <b>factory-farm</b> packaged meat, \"genetically manipulated\", a <b>patty</b> that doesn't look like an animal</li><li class=\"d1\">if you kill and clean it yourself → more <b>respect</b> for other living things</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience swaps stories: grandfather killing chickens and rabbits, killing a <b>hog</b> - \"food on the table\"</li></ul>"},{"t":809.12,"lv":2,"h":"Blood and the Eucharist","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ban on <b>drinking blood</b> is super clear through the OT</li><li class=\"d0\">Yet at every <b>Eucharist</b> we drink <b>Christ's blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John 6</b>: \"unless you eat my flesh and drink my blood\" → the Jews start to <b>leave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we don't appreciate how <b>shocking</b> - not just eating him but <b>drinking blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: blood sausage / blood pudding?</li><li class=\"d1\">technically <b>a violation</b> of the rule; maybe just <b>poverty</b> - \"gotta eat what you gotta eat\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If blood = life, then in the Eucharist <b>we receive Christ's life</b> within ourselves</li><li class=\"d0\">Making blood <b>sacred and taboo</b> in the OC helps reveal the <b>fulfillment of sacrifice</b> in the Eucharist</li></ul>"},{"t":965.29,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 16:1-3 - Timothy circumcised","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul and Silas left <b>Antioch</b>, went through <b>Syria and Cilicia</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now returning to churches founded in <b>Asia Minor</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:1</b> - <b>Derbe and Lystra</b>: disciple <b>Timothy</b>, son of a <b>Jewish believing woman</b>, father <b>Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">later recipient of <b>1 and 2 Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>first second-generation Christian</b> we meet</li><li class=\"d1\">mother raised him; everyone before was <b>adult convert</b></li><li class=\"d1\">father just a <b>Gentile</b> - nothing about believing, probably <b>pagan</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:2</b> - well spoken of by brethren at <b>Lystra and Iconium</b> (churches Paul founded last journey)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:3</b> - Paul wants him along, <b>circumcises him</b> because of the Jews in that region - all knew father was Greek</li></ul>"},{"t":1072.76,"lv":3,"h":"Contradiction with Acts 15?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Seems to <b>contradict</b> Acts 15 - Paul \"adamant\": <b>don't need to be circumcised</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now wants to train a young man as leader → has him circumcised</li><li class=\"d0\">Q1: <b>is Timothy a Gentile?</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>mother Jewish</b> → being Jewish goes by the <b>mother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so he is Jewish; father Gentile</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's strategy: <b>synagogue first</b>, preach, move out from there</li><li class=\"d1\">to those communities Timothy is an <b>uncircumcised Jew</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accusation: Paul not only says Gentiles needn't be circumcised, he tells <b>Jews not to circumcise children</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy likely uncircumcised because the <b>father wouldn't allow it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul isn't telling Jews to drop <b>laws and customs of our people</b> - doesn't want that accusation</li></ul>"},{"t":1204.55,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's consistent goal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why avoid accusation? His goal is to bring <b>Jews into the church too</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Uncircumcised Timothy = <b>stumbling block</b> → people don't hear the gospel</li><li class=\"d0\">Better to circumcise than <b>alienate</b></li><li class=\"d0\">1 Corinthians: \"<b>all things to all people</b>, that I might by all means save some\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: bring the <b>whole world</b> to Christ - <b>everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">making Gentiles become Jewish first = <b>obstacle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy's uncircumcision to Jews = <b>obstacle</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel already full of obstacles</li><li class=\"d1\">Messiah came, <b>Romans crucified him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every gathering we <b>eat his flesh and drink his blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Doesn't want to add one thing\" - no <b>ethnic</b>, no <b>religious</b> extras</li><li class=\"d0\">\"The gospel he's preaching - he wants to add <b>nothing else</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1338.99,"lv":3,"h":"Acts 16:4-5 - Decrees delivered","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:4</b> - delivered the <b>decrees</b> determined by apostles and elders at Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d0\">Letter from <b>James</b> sent to all the churches</li><li class=\"d1\">keep from <b>idolatry, sexual immorality, blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:5</b> - churches <b>strengthened in the faith</b>, <b>increased in number daily</b></li><li class=\"d1\">churches they planted growing continuously</li></ul>"},{"t":1373.59,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 16:6-10 - Spirit redirects to Macedonia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:6</b> - through <b>Phrygia and Galatia</b>; <b>forbidden by the Holy Spirit</b> to preach in <b>Asia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: aren't they in Asia? → <b>Asia Minor</b> vs <b>Asia proper</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Galatia more north; they went <b>south to north</b> through the middle of Turkey</li><li class=\"d1\">original plan: go further up into <b>Asia</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:7</b> - tried <b>Bithynia</b>, Spirit did not permit</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:8-9</b> - passing <b>Mysia</b>, down to <b>Troas</b>; <b>vision</b> to Paul at night</li><li class=\"d1\">man of <b>Macedonia</b>: \"Come over to Macedonia and help us\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:10</b> - \"immediately <b>we</b> sought to go to Macedonia\" - concluding the <b>Lord called us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Plans changed by <b>Spirit's prompting + dream</b> → <b>Macedonia</b>, north of Greece</li><li class=\"d1\">veering to the <b>west</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1435.51,"lv":3,"h":"The \"we\" - Luke joins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Notice the <b>change in pronoun</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: does \"we\" refer to Silas? → no, <b>first person all of a sudden</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul had the dream; then \"<b>we</b> decided to go\"</li><li class=\"d0\">St. Luke \"let slip\" that <b>he is now there too</b></li><li class=\"d1\">group: <b>Paul, Silas, Timothy, Luke</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1534.86,"lv":2,"h":"Who was St. Luke?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Covered in the <b>intro to Acts</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Physician</b></li><li class=\"d1\">different from today's doctors in the 1st c., \"not completely dissimilar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not a \"weird mystic throwing <b>chicken feathers</b>\" - <b>science as they understood it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">trying to understand body, disease, <b>relieving pain</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Therefore <b>very wealthy</b>, <b>very well-educated</b> - among the most educated in society</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman medical textbooks survive - <b>Galen</b> and others</li><li class=\"d1\">some good and still useful, some \"pretty crazy\" - but a <b>legitimate field of learning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Explains how he wrote <b>gospel + Acts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek style based on <b>Thucydides</b> and classical historians</li><li class=\"d1\">physician would have read widely: history, literature, <b>poetry</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1661.01,"lv":3,"h":"Luke's background and portraits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tradition: from <b>Antioch</b>, became Christian there, met <b>Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul spent several years preaching and teaching in Antioch</li><li class=\"d0\">Debate: was he <b>Jewish</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">geography of <b>Judea</b>, how he <b>transliterates Hebrew words</b> → used to conclude <b>not Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>Semiticisms</b> - sentence structure and grammar proper to <b>Aramaic, Syriac, Arabic</b>, not Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Most scholars now: ethnically <b>another Semitic people</b> - probably <b>Syriac</b>, living in Antioch</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a <b>Syrian Christian</b>, ethnically Syrian\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1765.41,"lv":3,"h":"Icons painted by Luke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Audience comment: two of <b>Luke's icons</b> still exist</li><li class=\"d1\">one on <b>Mount Athos</b>; other maybe in <b>Rome</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Good case beyond \"the church says so\"</li><li class=\"d1\">painted on <b>wax tablets</b> - how portraits were painted in the 1st c., <b>not long after</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't prove a particular person painted it, but <b>very good evidence</b> it goes back to that period</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel: tissue typing of bones under <b>St. Peter's Basilica</b> → a <b>Jewish man of the 1st c. AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"can't prove it's Peter - but how many Jewish men from the first century could it be?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">This passage = point where <b>Luke accompanies them</b> - larger traveling team</li></ul>"},{"t":1858.21,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 16:11-12 - Philippi","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:11-12</b> - sail from <b>Troas</b>, straight course to <b>Samothrace</b>, next day <b>Neapolis</b>, then <b>Philippi</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"foremost city of that part of Macedonia, a <b>colony</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"staying in that city for some days\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now it's all <b>\"we\"</b> - stays that way</li><li class=\"d0\">Northern Asia Minor → across to <b>Macedonia</b>, above Greece</li><li class=\"d0\">Many <b>Philippis</b>, like several <b>Antiochs</b> and many <b>Alexandrias</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Philip of Macedon</b>, father of <b>Alexander the Great</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: a <b>Roman</b> settlement? → yes, Roman colony, named for Philip anyway</li></ul>"},{"t":1926.05,"lv":2,"h":"Colonies and port cities","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Port city</b> on the <b>eastern</b> side → important though a recent colony</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans great at <b>roads and trade routes</b>, keeping the economy moving</li><li class=\"d1\">joke: \"if we have to come back and destroy you, we have a <b>nice neat road</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colony cities</b> = mostly <b>military veterans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after service + citizenship: granted <b>land</b> at the edge of territory</li><li class=\"d1\">veterans set up city, bring the <b>Roman way of life</b>, integrate area into empire</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel with the <b>US</b>: our money and culture \"transform it into a <b>mirror image</b> of us\"</li><li class=\"d1\">barbarous region → \"it all becomes Rome\" with money and trade</li></ul>"},{"t":2046.53,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Thessalonica and ports","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Thessalonica</b> the big port there - how far?</li><li class=\"d0\">Ports not \"destination cities\" like <b>London → New York</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't sail straight across the Mediterranean or <b>Aegean</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sailed <b>along the coast</b>, putting in at points along the way</li><li class=\"d1\">port = <b>travel stop</b> - commerce, sailors \"doing what sailors do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">many more ports than now</li><li class=\"d0\">Thessalonica or <b>Ephesus</b> might be the final destination; many stops between</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>Blythe, California</b></li><li class=\"d1\">midway between <b>Los Angeles and Phoenix</b>, middle of desert</li><li class=\"d1\">gas station → bigger gas station → <b>casino</b> → in ~20 years a huge travel stop</li><li class=\"d1\">similar with new port cities</li></ul>"},{"t":2174.41,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 16:13-15 - Lydia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Philippi = where they <b>begin in Macedonia</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:13</b> - <b>Sabbath</b>, out of the city to the <b>riverside</b> where prayer was customary; sat and spoke to the <b>women</b> who met there</li><li class=\"d1\">no organized <b>synagogue</b> - makes sense for a <b>Roman colony</b></li><li class=\"d1\">settled by Romans; only a <b>small Jewish community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">group primarily <b>women</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:14</b> - <b>Lydia</b>, seller of <b>purple</b> from <b>Thyatira</b>, worshipped God; Lord <b>opened her heart</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2249.17,"lv":3,"h":"Purple dye and Lydia's business","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Purple dye</b> from tiny <b>shellfish</b> shells, crushed for small quantities</li><li class=\"d1\">very expensive, <b>prized</b>, showy - why <b>emperors</b> wore purple</li><li class=\"d1\">whole purple garment = <b>very rich</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Mardi Gras purple and gold = <b>regal</b> colors</li><li class=\"d0\">Why a Jewish woman from Thyatira is in Philippi: <b>ply her trade</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Woman of some means</b></li><li class=\"d1\">difficult but not impossible for a woman in Roman society</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe <b>widow</b>, or unmarried and took over <b>parents' business</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"We don't know\" - maybe just a <b>remarkable woman</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2358.89,"lv":2,"h":"16:15 - Her household baptized","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>16:15</b> - she and her <b>household</b> baptized; \"if you have judged me faithful to the Lord, <b>come to my house and stay</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Household\" ≠ just kids</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman family unit <b>much larger</b>: parents, siblings, aunts, uncles, cousins</li><li class=\"d1\">plus <b>servants, slaves</b> - everyone who lived and worked there, maybe her <b>business</b> staff</li><li class=\"d1\">don't know how many or how related</li><li class=\"d0\">What matters: <b>whole household baptized</b> - happens a lot, more to come</li><li class=\"d0\">Our <b>individualistic</b> age → Protestant friends object to <b>baptizing babies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">baby can't make a <b>profession of faith</b>, own decision</li></ul>"},{"t":2467.96,"lv":2,"h":"Persona: identity by relationships","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not how people were thought of then</li><li class=\"d0\">Latin <i>persona</i> = literally <b>face</b> - your <b>public face</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Who you were = your <b>relationships</b> to others</li><li class=\"d1\">so many \"<b>Bar</b>-something\" - son of ... - defined by father</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans had four names</b>; only one personal</li><li class=\"d1\">father's, grandfather's, family name, given name</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Julius Caesar</b>'s given name = <b>Gaius</b>; <b>Cicero</b> = <b>Marcus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we remember them by <b>family names</b>; Julian clan → <b>Julio-Claudian dynasty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Don't read through <b>modern cultural eyes</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2570.85,"lv":2,"h":"Families decide together","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Families and social units made <b>decisions together</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Timothy: Jewish woman married a Gentile man → family converting does so <b>as a unit</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mixed households</b> - more tension then than now</li><li class=\"d1\">tension mostly with <b>Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two pagans marrying → just <b>smush</b> household gods together</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't work with a <b>Jewish</b> person - won't worship spouse's gods</li><li class=\"d0\">Marriage of Jew to Gentile, esp. a woman: \"not favored by any means\", sometimes tolerated</li><li class=\"d0\">Two dynamics to expect</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>whole household</b> becomes Christian as a unit</li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>one person</b> converts and is <b>cast out</b> of the family</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm only going to worship Jesus Christ. You're out of the family\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2708.78,"lv":3,"h":"Roman hearth and household gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lydia invites them into her home</li><li class=\"d0\">Roman home: <b>hearth</b> in the center</li><li class=\"d1\">no indoor heating, no running hot water → kept burning</li><li class=\"d1\">why <b>Vesta</b>, goddess of the hearth, so important</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Household gods</b> - idols/images at the hearth</li><li class=\"d1\">family gathered there for <b>prayer and worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gods viewed as <b>part of the family</b> and community</li><li class=\"d0\">True of <b>cities</b> too - city's gods part of the community</li><li class=\"d1\">source of <b>persecution</b>: \"I'm not worshiping <b>Athena</b> anymore - she's a <b>demon</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">neighbors: she brings <b>prosperity</b>; you'll bring her <b>anger</b> on all of us → \"get rid of you\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2827.81,"lv":2,"h":"Home icons and the prayer corner","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Within Scripture, converts <b>remove household idols</b> → replace with <b>iconography</b></li><li class=\"d1\">images of <b>Christ, his mother, the saints</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gather there to pray and worship (more at <b>1 Corinthians</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Origin of the Orthodox <b>prayer corner / home altar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">place set apart: <b>icons, holy water</b>, gather as a family</li><li class=\"d1\">goes back to the <b>early church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everybody included, <b>kids</b> too; decision made together, not forced</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"get baptized or I <b>fire you</b> from my purple business\"</li><li class=\"d1\">nor a <b>vote</b> - \"write it on a piece of paper and put it in a hat\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Lydia becomes <b>important figure</b> in Philippi</li><li class=\"d1\">her household = <b>home base</b> for the forming church</li></ul>"},{"t":2934.17,"lv":2,"h":"Why women and slaves converted","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"An <b>inordinate number</b> of early converts were <b>women and slaves</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity = only religion saying <b>slaves, women, children</b> equal in value</li><li class=\"d1\">every human <b>created in God's image</b>, equal in God's sight</li><li class=\"d1\">Judaism too? \"Yeah - unless you're a <b>Gentile</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Powerful: tell someone <b>slave for three generations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God who created the universe <b>loves you</b>, as important to him as <b>Caesar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">follow Christ → better off than Caesar, who must <b>give an account</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Other religions: misfortune = <b>cursed by God</b>, so <b>curse them too</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"stomp on them a little more to show you agree with God\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>caste system</b> in India</li></ul>"},{"t":3044.87,"lv":3,"h":"Rome's brutality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rome \"not that much different\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of the empire legally <b>non-persons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no rights unless a <b>Roman citizen</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jews</b> lower than slaves</li><li class=\"d0\">Why they could crucify <b>Jesus</b> with no real charges</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pilate</b> (from the <b>Gospel of John</b> study)</li><li class=\"d1\">~20 years before, feared a <b>riot at Passover</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crucified <b>150 random Jews</b> along the road, grabbed off the street</li><li class=\"d1\">so incoming pilgrims would see them dying</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans considered them \"<b>animals</b> walking around on their land\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3102.27,"lv":2,"h":"Going the extra mile","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Going the extra mile</b>\" - Jesus: if compelled one mile, go two</li><li class=\"d0\">Roman soldier could take the <b>pack</b> off your back: \"carry this for the next mile\"</li><li class=\"d1\">refuse → they could <b>kill you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no investigation, nothing</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Thank God we haven't had to experience that firsthand\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Society <b>brutal</b>; not all human beings seen as equally valuable</li><li class=\"d1\">victim = \"<b>pathetic</b> and you deserve it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3182.24,"lv":3,"h":"God loves you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul preaches to <b>women</b>, <b>slaves</b>, the <b>poor</b>, <b>Jews</b> abused daily - \"<b>God loves you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Nobody thought <b>Zeus</b> loved them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">joke: \"Nobody was singing, <b>What a Friend We Have in Zeus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Zeus angry, threw <b>lightning bolts</b>; you kept him <b>pacified</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even the <b>emperor</b> wasn't loved</li><li class=\"d0\">But the <b>creator of the earth</b> loves you</li><li class=\"d1\">cares what's happening to you, what you've gone through today, the <b>indignities</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's a <b>powerful thing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Big force behind why so many <b>women, slaves, peasants, underclass</b> (even Roman citizens) convert <b>in droves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">experiencing the <b>love of God for the first time</b> when it is proclaimed</li></ul>"}]});
